-- | Lexes, and the **parser** rejects it with `RDX0102`.
--
-- Named `parse_error` and not `syntax_error`, because the distinction is real
-- and this fixture was wrong about it once. The first version was `print (1`,
-- an unbalanced delimiter — which is `RDX0012`, a **lexical** code, since the
-- lexer tracks bracket depth. Plan 40 §10.3 found the same thing and split its
-- test in two for it; this row re-earned the lesson rather than reading it.
--
-- `else` with no `if` is the parser's complaint, which is what `redox_build`
-- and `redox_fmt` need: a program that reaches the parser and stops there.
--
-- It was `then 1` until plan 55 leaf 7 freed that word. A retired keyword is
-- an **identifier**, so `then 1` became an ordinary call to an undefined
-- function — it parses, and the complaint moves to rustc, which is the
-- opposite of what this fixture asserts. `else` is reserved and has no
-- production outside an `if`, so it stops where `then` used to.
main : ()
main =
  else 1
